## Server actions

Export an `action()` to mount a validated endpoint at `POST /__sp/api/v1/actions/<name>` (and expose it to `"use client"` components and the typed client):

```ts
import { action, integer, HttpError, db } from "spacepress";

export const archiveTask = action({
  input: { id: integer() },
  access: "editor",
  idempotent: true,
  async handler({ id }) {
    const [row] = await db.update(tasks).set({ done: true }).where({ id }).returning();
    if (!row) throw new HttpError(404, "not_found", "Task not found");
    return row;
  },
});
```

Set `readonly: true` or `destructive: true` to annotate the generated WordPress Ability for AI agents, or `signature` to verify incoming HMAC webhooks.

## Background jobs

Export a `job()` and enqueue it with `jobs.<name>.enqueue(input, { delay?, key? })`. Jobs run asynchronously on WordPress Action Scheduler, commit atomically with the enclosing database transaction, support automatic `retries`, and can guard non-idempotent side effects with `once(key, fn)`.

## Transactional hooks & WordPress hooks

- Chain `.on({ beforeCreate, afterCreate, beforeUpdate, afterUpdate, beforeDelete, afterDelete, afterPublish })` onto any collection or table. Throwing aborts the request and rolls back the transaction.
- Place typed WordPress core filters/actions in `sp/wp-hooks/<hook>.ts` using `Handler<"...">` from `"php/wordpress"`.

## Custom HTTP routes & private files

- Create `sp/app/<path>/route.ts` exporting `GET`, `POST`, `PUT`, `PATCH`, or `DELETE` plus `export const guard`.
- Define private upload buckets with `files({ accept, maxBytes, access })` backed by local disk or S3-compatible storage (`uploads: { backend: "s3", ... }`). Loading a `file(bucket)` column via `with` yields a 5-minute signed URL.

## Visitor-scoped vs trusted database APIs

Import named kinds from `"spacepress:db"` for visitor-scoped operations that automatically enforce access rules, draft visibility, and field masks:

```ts
import { posts } from "spacepress:db";

const published = await posts.list({
  where: { tags: { has: "release" } },
  orderBy: { publishedAt: "desc" },
  with: { cover: true },
  limit: 20,
});

// Explicit system escalation when needed:
const all = await posts.system.list({ limit: 100 });
```

Inside hooks, jobs, and trusted server actions, you can also use the Drizzle-inspired `db` API from `"spacepress"`:

```ts
import { db } from "spacepress";

const row = await db.query.posts.findFirst({ where: { slug: "hello" } });
const [created] = await db.insert(tasks).values({ title: "Ship v1" }).returning();
```

## Declarative access rules

The role ladder is ordered: `guest < subscriber < author < editor < admin`. Naming a role admits that role and every higher role.

- Shorthands: `"anyone"`, `"authenticated"`, `"self"`, role name, or boolean.
- Functions: `(ctx) => boolean | AccessFilter` — returning a filter restricts `read`, `update`, and `delete` queries to matching rows (non-matching rows return `404`).
- Field masks: `.access({ read, create, update })` on any column strips or refuses unauthorized fields across REST, pages, and client queries.

## Authoring templates in `emails/`

Write transactional email templates using `react-email` components under `emails/*.tsx`:

```tsx
import { Body, Button, Html, Text } from "react-email";

export default function Invitation({ name, url }: { name: string; url: string }) {
  return (
    <Html lang="en">
      <Body>
        <Text>Hello, {name}!</Text>
        <Button href={url}>Accept invitation</Button>
      </Body>
    </Html>
  );
}
```

## Transactional sending

Call `mail.render(Template, props)` to produce `{ html, text }`, then queue delivery with `mail.send()`:

```ts
const rendered = mail.render(Invitation, { name, url });
await mail.send({
  to: recipientEmail,
  subject: "You're invited",
  html: rendered.html,
  text: rendered.text,
});
```

`mail.send()` queues the message inside the current database transaction and dispatches it after commit with retries — if a hook or action rolls back, no email is sent.

## Scaffolding a storefront

Add a complete WooCommerce storefront to any project with:

```bash
npx spacepress add shop
```

## Declarative store & catalog builders

Export `store()`, `product()`, `productCategory()`, `productTag()`, and `order()` from `sp/shop.ts` (re-exported in `spacepress.config.ts`):

```ts
import { store, product, productCategory, productTag, order } from "spacepress";

export const shop = store({
  currency: "EUR",
  country: "PL",
  sellTo: ["PL", "DE"],
  shipping: [
    {
      name: "Poland",
      countries: ["PL"],
      methods: [
        { type: "flatRate", title: "Courier", cost: "15.00" },
        { type: "freeShipping", title: "Free over 200", minimum: "200.00" },
      ],
    },
  ],
  payments: ["cod"],
});

export const products = product({ sync: "content/products/*.md", url: "/products/:slug" });
export const categories = productCategory({ url: "/shop/:path" });
export const tags = productTag();
export const orders = order();
```

## Optimistic cart client & React hooks

Whenever a project exports `store()`, `spacepress build` emits `commerce.ts`, `cart-store.ts`, and `cart-react.ts` in `sp/_generated/client/`. Call `createCartHooks(React)` to obtain `useCart()` and `useCartCount()` with optimistic item quantity updates and automatic rollback on failure.

